Severity class for the 2022 record: **widespread/systematic repression**, with especially severe Xinjiang and Hong Kong patterns, plus coercive zero-COVID enforcement and protest crackdowns. The record does not show a 1B-style mass-terror floor in 2022, but it is far below protective or restrained governance.

**New Evidence**
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"OHCHR Assessment of human rights concerns in the Xinjiang Uyghur Autonomous Region, People's Republic of China","publisher":"UN Office of the High Commissioner for Human Rights","publication_date":"2022-08-31","url":"https://www.ohchr.org/sites/default/files/documents/countries/2022-08-31/22-08-31-final-assesment.pdf","claim":"OHCHR concluded that serious human rights violations were committed in Xinjiang under counter-terrorism and counter-extremism strategies, producing severe and undue restrictions and large-scale arbitrary deprivation of liberty with scant independent oversight.","locator":"pages 44-45, paragraphs 143-149","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary UN assessment based on documentary review, official materials, interviews, and open-source corroboration.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 assessment of the Xinjiang policy environment relevant to Xi's domestic safety record.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but strong: Xi presided over the party-state that set the counter-terror framework and security posture.","contrary_evidence":["The report does not assign personal criminal responsibility to Xi.","It focuses on the policy framework and documented pattern rather than individual orders."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.4","3B.7","3B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"China: Further information: Missing and arbitrarily detained in Xinjiang","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-07-29","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/documents/asa17/5905/2022/en/","claim":"Amnesty said 48 additional people from predominantly Muslim ethnic groups had been interned or sentenced without a fair trial, bringing its campaign total to 120, and described the pattern as mass detention and torture affecting an estimated 1 million or more people since 2017.","locator":"paragraphs 1-2 / lines 67-69","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct Amnesty documentation with explicit figures and allegations, consistent with other independent reporting.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Strong 2022 fit for the inherited Xinjiang safety baseline and ongoing detention pattern.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but material under Xi's leadership; the allegations concern the security system he controls.","contrary_evidence":["This is advocacy reporting, not a court finding.","It uses estimated totals and campaign framing rather than a full official census."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.7","3B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"China: Xi Jinping’s continued tenure as leader a disaster for human rights","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-10-23","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2022/10/china-xi-jinpings-continued-tenure-as-leader-a-disaster-for-human-rights/","claim":"Amnesty said Xi's decade in power had featured sweeping arbitrary detentions, a nationwide crackdown on freedom of expression and association, crimes against humanity against Muslims in Xinjiang, and escalating repression in Hong Kong, with censorship tightened during politically sensitive periods.","locator":"lines 63-75","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct contemporaneous Amnesty statement summarizing the 2022 rights environment under Xi.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Excellent fit for the 2022 political environment and Xi's third-term consolidation.","ruler_attribution":"Direct at the leadership level; it explicitly attributes the record to Xi's rule.","contrary_evidence":["This is a rights-advocacy judgment, not a judicial verdict.","It compresses a decade-long pattern into a summary of 2022 conditions."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.3","3B.5","3B.8","3B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Report 2023: China","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023-01","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/china","claim":"HRW reported that China imposed repeated, unpredictable lockdowns on hundreds of millions of people in 2022, in some cases using barriers to prevent people from leaving homes, and that people reported trouble accessing food and medical care, sometimes leading to deaths.","locator":"lines 81-83, 121-123","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent human-rights monitoring with a concise year-end country chapter.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Strong retrospective fit for the 2022 domestic-safety environment and zero-COVID enforcement.","ruler_attribution":"Direct in system-level terms: these were state policies under Xi's leadership, though carried out by local officials.","contrary_evidence":["HRW is summarizing a national pattern and may understate local variation.","The page does not isolate Xi's personal orders from local implementation."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.6","3B.9","3B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Report 2023: China","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023-01","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/china","claim":"HRW said Hong Kong authorities under the Beijing-imposed security framework arrested people for national-security-related offenses, censored politically sensitive material, increased police surveillance, and constrained unions, universities, and media.","locator":"lines 90-101","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent reporting in a major rights-monitoring annual review.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Good fit for 2022 because it captures the year-end state of repression in Hong Kong under Xi-era rule.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but strong: Hong Kong policy was shaped by the Xi-led central government.","contrary_evidence":["The page covers Hong Kong as a separate legal system, so attribution to Xi is structural rather than a single command.","Some actions were taken by Hong Kong authorities, not the mainland government directly."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.5","3B.8","3B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"China: Government must not detain peaceful protesters as unprecedented demonstrations break out across the country","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-11-27","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2022/11/china-lockdown-protests-covid/","claim":"Amnesty said the protests were peaceful, that authorities were likely to respond with censorship, surveillance, police force, and mass arrests, and that officials should not penalize people for protesting lockdown harms.","locator":"lines 63-82","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous rights-monitoring statement with specific claims, though some arrest details were from online sources.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Excellent fit for the late-November 2022 protest wave after the Urumqi fire.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but relevant to Xi's domestic-security posture because the crackdown occurred under his leadership.","contrary_evidence":["Amnesty could not independently verify every video or arrest detail.","The statement includes predictive language about likely police actions as well as observed events."],"lenses":["3B.8","3B.9","3B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Blank sheets of paper become symbol of defiance in China protests","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-11-27","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/coronavirus/blank-sheets-of-paper-become-symbol-of-defiance-in-china-protests-2952307","claim":"Reuters reported that protesters used blank sheets of paper to evade censorship or arrest, describing the demonstrations as a rare, widespread outpouring of dissent and saying room for dissent had been all but eliminated under Xi.","locator":"lines 432-434, 453-459","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters is strong for contemporaneous event reporting, though this is a republished copy.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Excellent fit for the protest-wave and censorship lens in November 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct to Xi-era domestic control conditions, though not to a single named order.","contrary_evidence":["The article is descriptive protest reporting, not a legal finding.","It does not establish the full chain of command behind every arrest or censorship act."],"lenses":["3B.3","3B.8","3B.9","3B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Chinese authorities seek out COVID protesters","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-11-28","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/coronavirus/china-police-out-in-numbers-to-prevent-more-covid-protests-2953228","claim":"Reuters reported that Beijing police called some protesters in for written accounts of their activities and that police remained out in numbers in the streets after the protests.","locator":"lines 434-439","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters reporting with direct witness accounts, though not all details were independently verified in the article.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Excellent fit for the immediate post-protest crackdown in late November 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct at the system level: the police response occurred under Xi's domestic-security authority.","contrary_evidence":["The article does not prove formal detention in every case it describes.","It captures a narrow moment and does not quantify the full crackdown."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.8","3B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"China set to loosen COVID curbs after week of historic protests","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-12-01","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/world-news/china-set-to-loosen-covid-curbs-after-week-of-historic-protests-2955191","claim":"Reuters said China was preparing to ease quarantine protocols and reduce mass testing after anger over the world's toughest curbs fueled widespread protests, with some cities already lifting lockdowns.","locator":"lines 421-426","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters reporting of a clear policy shift, though based partly on sources and not a formal government rationale.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Excellent fit for the immediate policy response to the late-2022 protest wave.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but important: it reflects policy under Xi's leadership, even if local authorities implemented the change.","contrary_evidence":["Reuters says officials did not publicly acknowledge the protests as the driver.","Policy easing did not end the broader repression pattern."],"lenses":["3B.6","3B.9","3B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"COVID-19 response further optimized with 10 new measures","publisher":"State Council / Xinhua","publication_date":"2022-12-08","url":"https://english.www.gov.cn/statecouncil/ministries/202212/08/content_WS63913c92c6d0a757729e4135.html","claim":"China's State Council said the new COVID measures were meant to replace one-size-fits-all steps, prohibit arbitrary expansion of high-risk areas, reduce mass testing, allow home quarantine in some cases, and keep basic medical and social functions operating.","locator":"lines 45-62","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official policy circular with concrete operational changes and dated implementation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Excellent fit for the December 2022 policy rollback after protests.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but meaningful system-level attribution under Xi's leadership.","contrary_evidence":["This is a policy optimization, not an admission of abuse.","The measures came after major public pressure and did not erase earlier harms."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.6","3B.9","3B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Foreign Ministry Spokesperson’s Remarks on the Human Rights Council’s Vote Against a Draft Decision on Xinjiang","publisher":"Ministry of Foreign Affairs of the People's Republic of China","publication_date":"2022-10-07","url":"https://www.fmprc.gov.cn/eng/xw/fyrbt/fyrbt/202405/t20240530_11349759.html","claim":"China rejected the UN Human Rights Council Xinjiang decision as disinformation, insisted Xinjiang was about countering violent terrorism and separatism, and said there had been no violent terrorist incident in Xinjiang for more than five years.","locator":"lines 67-75","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct official response showing the government's own justification and denial posture.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Good 2022 fit for the regime's public defense of Xinjiang policy after the UN assessment.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but strong: this is the Xi-led state's public position.","contrary_evidence":["The statement is self-justifying and directly conflicts with OHCHR and NGO findings.","It can show regime narrative without proving actual safety."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.6","3B.10"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**
- I considered roughly 30 documents across OHCHR, Amnesty, Human Rights Watch, Reuters, and Chinese official statements.
- I kept the smallest set that covered the main 3B themes: Xinjiang detention and oversight failure, protest policing, zero-COVID coercion, Hong Kong repression, and the government’s own denials and partial rollback.
- I rejected duplicate Reuters syndications where the same story appeared in multiple wrappers.
- I rejected 2023-2026 retrospective pieces when they added no new 2022 factual content beyond earlier sources.
- I rejected sources focused mainly on economics, foreign policy, or nuclear/security doctrine because they belong in other chapters.
- I rejected broad opinion columns and low-specificity summaries when a primary or stronger rights-monitoring source was available.
- I did not re-fetch structured local priors such as Freedom House or V-Dem because the local facts already supplied the relevant baseline and the instructions forbid re-fetching those datasets for this scope.

**Remaining Gaps**
- No public 2022 source shows Xi personally ordering specific arrests, torture, or detention decisions; attribution is therefore mostly system-level and structural.
- The record is strongest for Xinjiang and the protest crackdown. It is weaker on due process, complaint handling, and independent remedy because I found no credible 2022 evidence of effective oversight or sanctions against abusers.
- The protest-crackdown evidence is strong on harassment and surveillance, but the full scale of detentions and case outcomes remains underreported.
- More search would only help if you want direct command-chain evidence or province-by-province implementation data. It is unlikely to change the overall severity class.
